\par\par \b \uc2 The Morning Ledger: Embracing Differences\b0\par\par\uc2 978 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2026\par 13:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Former CFO's Advice for Successor: Embrace the Differences By Walden Siew | WSJ Leadership Institute\par \par \uc2 Good morning, CFOs. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's \uc2 James von Moltke\uc2  on transformation and his best parting advice; the state of play in the Strait of Hormuz; plus, how AI giants are using AI in their own offices.\par \par \uc2 Kristin Broughton recently sat down with \uc2 James von Moltke\uc2 , president and former CFO of \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , to talk about his tenure at the German lender. Kristin writes for today's newsletter:\par \par \uc2 Von Moltke\uc2 , who served as CFO for nine years before stepping down in March, oversaw a significant transformation of the bank, and also helped it weather economic and market shocks, including the pandemic. \uc2 Raja Akram\uc2 , previously deputy CFO at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , succeeded him as CFO at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here's a bit more of our conversation, condensed and edited for clarity:\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about the 2019 transformation, because it was so big. Are there areas where you think the bank cut too far or did too much, or areas where the bank could have done more and should have done more?\par \par \uc2 I do feel that we did as much as the organization could absorb. Which isn't to say we didn't make mistakes along the way-we did. I do think that the execution discipline that we sort of built over the time was immensely important.\par \par \uc2 It was important both for the ability to deliver on our plans and pursue the associated transformations. But also that execution discipline was an important element of being able to navigate all those exogenous shocks that took place over the time.\par \par \uc2 Could you ever see Deutsche adding business lines or acquiring another bank in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 I wouldn't rule it out forever. Adding business lines, I would doubt, except the ones that are very much adjacent to the things that we already do. It's hard to speculate about M&A. I don't see that on the radar screen.\par \par \uc2 What's the most important piece of parting advice that you've given to your successor?\par \par \uc2 My advice to Raja is, embrace the differences. He and I both crossed the Atlantic to take this role as CFO of \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's very easy to make that change and then scratch your head and ask, 'Why do people do things so differently over here?' And sometimes you just have to embrace that difference and enjoy it for some of its unique features.\par \par \uc2 To read the full interview, click here .\par \par \uc2 The Day Ahead\par \par \uc2 Earnings\par \par \uc2 Darden Restaurants FedEx Freight Holding McCormick\par \par \uc2 Economic Indicators\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Economic Analysis\uc2  releases the personal consumption expenditures price index for May.\par \par \uc2 The Census Bureau releases the durable goods report for May.\par \par \uc2 What Else Matters to CFOs\par \par \uc2 President Trump is pulling from a familiar Democratic playbook as he wrestles with sticky gasoline prices: blaming Big Oil .\par \par \uc2 Corporate response: Some oil executives are privately critical of the president's comments and explained the fuel supply chain is a slow-moving machine.\par \par \uc2 During the U.S. war against Iran, Trump repeatedly promised that prices at the pump would fall quickly once the conflict ended. But 10 days after he announced an agreement with Iran, the price of a gallon of gasoline still hovers around $3.93-nearly $1 higher than before the hostilities broke out.\par \par \uc2 What's the State of Play in the Strait of Hormuz?\par \par \uc2 Other headlines\par \par \uc2 Big Banks Ace an Easier Annual Stress Test\par \par \uc2 See How AI Giants Are Using AI in Their Own Offices\par \par \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  Veterans' Startup Seeks to Help Scientists Develop Their Own AI\par \par \uc2 New Drugs Are Replacing Chemo for Aggressive Breast Cancer\par \par \uc2 Forget Work. Passive Income Is the New American Dream.\par \par \uc2 The Data-Center Boom Is Sparking a Third Wave of Inflation\par \par \uc2 Will Anyone Buy This Cheap EV Truck With Hand-Crank Windows and No Radio?\par \par \uc2 Podcast: First an Energy Crisis. Now El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o?\par \par \uc2 Earnings wrapup\par \par \uc2 Micron Shares Jump as Chip Shortage Projected to Last Beyond 2027\par \par \uc2 McCormick\uc2  Posts Higher Sales Amid Combination With \uc2 Unilever\par \par \uc2 For more earnings news, click here .\par \par \uc2 Cannes 2026\par \par \uc2 Economic uncertainty is the issue of our time, largely driven by technological change, according to \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  Chief Business Officer Mark Lobosco, speaking at the annual Cannes Lions festival this week.\par \par \uc2 Lobosco noted that the so-called creator economy has been an engine of growth for \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . And overall global hiring is currently running 20% to 30% below pre-pandemic levels, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  sector remains a major bright spot.\par \par \uc2 The AI economy also has generated over 1.3 million new jobs in the past year, driving the emergence of "new collar" roles that combine technical expertise with vocational skills for projects like building data centers.\par \par \uc2 For a look at the full interview, click here .\par \par \uc2 Read on to see Nasdaq's CMO and other videos from the WSJ Leadership Institute's coverage of the festival, including:\par \par \uc2 Nasdaq Chief Marketing Officer Brian Buckley, on why companies must remain deeply authentic\par \par \uc2 to their roots and DNA. \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  Chief Marketing Officer Jessica Jensen, on the platform's recent push into video\par \par \uc2 to attract Gen Z users and the launch of a new creator marketplace. Big Number About Us\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal's CFO Journal offers corporate leaders and professionals CFO analysis, advice and commentary to make informed decisions. We cover topics including corporate tax, accounting, regulation, capital markets, management and strategy.\par \par \uc2 Follow us on X @WSJCFO . The WSJ CFO Journal Team comprises reporters Kristin Broughton , Mark Maurer and Jennifer Williams , and Bureau Chief Walden Siew .\par \par \uc2 You can reach us by replying to any newsletter, or email Walden at [walden.siew@wsj.com].\par \par \uc2 This article is a text version of a Wall Street Journal newsletter published earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 25, 2026 07:15 ET (11:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260625em6p001js\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
